Durarara!! AU
Chapter 26 – Part 2
Curtis kept his voice even.
“We may not know each other yet. But I’ll stay.” Long black braids shifted against the blue blazer. “Anri is worried about you. She still looks for you.”
Mika’s face changed.
Not the reconstruction — the girl under it. The name Anri did what Mika had done, only sharper, because it came with a hallway and a friendship she had already admitted to using.
“…She still looks?” It came out small, almost disbelieving. “After I made her into a door to get to him? After I disappeared and left her with nothing but a hole where her friend used to stand?”
A short, wrecked breath.
“I thought she stopped. I thought she had to stop. People look for a while and then the city eats the search.” Her fingers tightened on the strap. “If she’s still looking, then I didn’t just run from Seiji and the rider. I left her in it.”
She stared at the carpet.
“Does she know about this?” A tiny gesture at her own face, the scar, the suite. “About what I let them do? If you tell her I’m here as this, she won’t see Mika. She’ll see the thing I became to keep a boy who never wanted her friend.”
Curtis did not lie.
“She knows you’re missing. She knows you were tied to Seiji. I asked her your name tonight. I didn’t tell her where you are. That’s your call when you’re ready — not mine to dump on her through a text.”
Mika nodded once, shaky.
“…Don’t tell her yet. Please. If I see her like this I’ll break. If she sees me like this she’ll think I died and something else came back wearing my voice.” A thinner beat. “But if she’s still looking… tell her I’m alive. Just that. If you can do it without giving her this room. I owe her that much even if I can’t walk into her arms tonight.”
She looked at him again.
“You said you’ll stay. Then stay while I figure out how to be Mika for Anri without handing her a reconstructed stranger and calling it a reunion.”
Curtis glanced at the locked phone, at Anri’s thread, at the girl on the far bed.
Alive.
Not the hotel.
Not the face.
That was a message he could send without setting the whole city on fire.
Whether he should send it yet sat in the quiet beside the promise to stay.
room that had been carrying too much weight.
“Hey. I’m not saying spring the whole truth on her.” Long black braids shifted as he tipped his chin toward her bag. “Why not leave her a message and tell her you’re okay. You don’t have to spill everything all at once. Things get messy that way.”
Mika’s mouth twitched — not quite a smile, close enough to count.
“You’re safe right now.” He glanced at the suite like he was seeing the minibar and the two beds for the first time as something other than a hiding place. “Are you thirsty or hungry? Let me know. Since the room’s paid for and it’s fancy. When in Rome, am I right?”
The girl stared at him for a second, then let out a breath that was almost a laugh.
“…You’re weird.” Not unkind. “People chase me, you hide me, you tell me I matter, and then you ask if I want room service like this is a school trip.”
A short pause.
“I can text Anri. Just I’m okay. I’m safe. Don’t look in the bad places. Not this address. Not this face. Not Seiji.” Her fingers found her phone and did not unlock it yet. “Hungry. I think. I don’t remember the last time I ate like a person instead of like someone running.”
She glanced at the second bed, then at him.
“If I order food, you have to eat too. I’m not sitting here being the only one who looks like a problem while you stand by the door like a guard. That’s… worse, somehow.”
Curtis huffed another quiet chuckle.
“Deal. Fancy hotel. Two beds. Room service. You send Anri okay and nothing that gets a Black Rider on the elevator.”
He moved to the desk binder like a boy who had originally wanted to build Gameboys and had ended up hosting a missing girl in a high-roller suite.
Outside, the city kept hunting a scar.
Inside, Mika Harima opened her messages to Anri with shaking thumbs —
and for the first time since the corner, the next step was a menu instead of a chase.
Curtis took a spare keycard from the folder and held it out.
“I’m heading downstairs for the food.” Long black braids shifted as he set the card in her palm. “I got a spare. Call me if you feel unsafe. I will come back no matter what. I’m only going to be gone a moment.”
He put his number on the nightstand beside her bag, written clear, no nickname, no extra story.
Mika looked at the card like it was heavier than plastic.
“…You’re leaving me the door.” Not quite panic. Surprise. People who hid her usually kept the only key. “And your number. And you’re telling me to use both.”
A short breath.
“Okay. I’ll lock it after you. I won’t open for anyone who isn’t you. Not even if they say Seiji. Not even if they say Anri.” Her fingers closed around the card. “If I’m not okay I’ll call. Don’t— don’t take the long way. The rider finds people in lobbies too.”
She glanced at his face, at the mismatched eyes that had already seen too much of her.
“Come back. You said you’d stay. Going downstairs still counts as leaving.” A thinner beat, almost dry. “Bring something sweet. If I’m going to sit in a fancy room wearing my real name, I want cake or I’m filing a complaint.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged.
“Cake. Got it. Chain the door.”
He stepped into the hall.
Behind him, Mika Harima waited until the latch clicked, then put the chain on with both hands, keycard on the bedspread, his number facing up —
and listened to the corridor like the city might try the handle the second the foreign student was gone.
The chain held.
Mika sat on the far bed with the keycard in her lap and Curtis’s number facing the ceiling, and the suite suddenly felt too big for one reconstructed girl.
Why is he so kind?
The thought did not arrive soft. It arrived suspicious, then tired, then worse — honest.
He just met me. Got me off the street. Offered actually good advice. And now he’s off to get me something to eat.
Seiji had made her feel chosen once, in a way that rewired everything after. Then the looking had narrowed. The neck. The face. The name that was not hers. Love as a spotlight that only stayed if she stood in the exact shape he wanted.
It was always one-sided with Seiji. But then I became Celty.
She touched the scar without meaning to.
But he sees me as Mika. And treats me like… a person.
Not a head.
Not a substitute.
Not a missing poster.
Not a thing to put back in a lab.
A person who got a second bed, a spare key, a warning not to open for Seiji’s name, and a promise of cake from a boy who had no reason to spend money on her except that she had crashed into his arms and said help me.
Mika stared at the door.
If this was leverage, it was a strange kind. People who wanted to use her did not leave her the only way out and then go downstairs.
“…Don’t get used to it,” she whispered to the empty room, and did not believe herself.
She unlocked her phone.
Anri’s thread sat there like a hallway she was not ready to walk down in this face.
I’m okay. I’m safe.
Her thumbs hovered.
Downstairs, a foreign student with mismatched eyes was buying cake for a girl the whole city wanted to collect.
On the fifth floor, Mika Harima tried to remember the last time kindness had not arrived with a condition —
and could not.
The knock was his — two short, then the key.
Curtis came back in with bags and a box and the particular look of someone who had talked a hotel kitchen into taking him seriously. Long black braids shifted against his Raira uniform; he glanced down at the white shirt and tie like he had only just remembered what he was wearing.
“Hmm. I guess this is on me for not packing extra clothes.” A shrug. “Oh well.”
He set the box on the table between the two beds and nudged it toward her.
“Here, Mika. Strawberry. The chef recommended it to me.”
She stared at the box, then at him, then at the fact that he had said Mika again like it was ordinary.
“…You actually brought cake.” It came out thinner than a joke. She pulled the box closer with both hands, white cap dipping as if the strawberry glaze might vanish if she looked too hard. “I was half sure you’d come back with a lecture and a vending-machine sandwich.”
A short breath.
“Thank you.” Real. Awkward. She popped the lid and the room smelled like sugar instead of running. “You can sit. You look like a student who fought a whole city and then went to class anyway. Eat some before I decide this is a trap made of pastry.”
She hesitated, fork hovering.
“…You called me Mika when you walked in. Like it wasn’t a test.” Her eyes flicked up to his. “Keep doing that. And if the chef asked who it was for, you better not have said Celty.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged.
“I said it was for a friend who had a long day.”
Mika looked at the strawberry cake like the word friend was more dangerous than the rider —
and for the first time since the alley, she took a bite.
Curtis smiled.
He took the other bed the way he had promised — his, not hers — and lay on his back with his shoes still on, long black braids spilling over the fancy pillow. From his pocket he pulled the folded flyer, bright and a little crushed from a day that had started with Namie and ended with strawberry cake.
Build your own Gameboy!
Take home a free game!
He looked at it like it belonged to a different boy.
“Next time, I guess.”
The words were light. The memorial box under them was not.
Mika glanced over from her cake, fork paused.
“…You were going somewhere. Before I hit you.” Not a question. She could see it in the way he held the paper. “A workshop.”
“Yeah.” Curtis let the flyer rest on his chest. “Build two. Keep one. Put one in a box for someone who isn’t here to play it.” A short breath at the ceiling. “Street had other plans.”
Mika looked at the flyer, then at the boy who had hidden her, fed her, called her by her name, and was now mourning a plastic console like it was a date he had broken with the dead.
“…Sorry,” she said, quieter. “For crashing into your next time.”
Curtis huffed a small sound that was almost a laugh.
“Don’t be. The Gameboy’ll wait. You wouldn’t have.”
He folded the flyer again and tucked it away, eyes on the ceiling of a suite he had not planned to buy.
Downstairs, Ikebukuro kept hunting a scar.
On the fifth floor, a missing girl ate strawberry cake, and a foreign student postponed a memorial —
and for a little while, next time was allowed to mean morning.
Curtis tipped his head on the pillow, flyer already put away.
“There’s always next time for this.” Long black braids shifted against the hotel white. “You’re a person who can’t be replaced. So don’t worry about it.”
Mika’s fork stopped halfway to the cake.
The sentence sat in the suite differently than the speeches. Smaller. Cleaner. A Gameboy workshop measured against a girl and found wanting without making her into a debt.
“…You can’t just say things like that while I’m eating,” she muttered, but her shoulders had eased. “It’s cheating.”
Curtis reached into the bag from downstairs and pulled out a stick of dango, inspecting it with the same seriousness he had given Namie’s childhood and a backfiring gun.
“Hmm. These look good.”
He bit one like the day had earned him something ordinary. The fancy room smelled like strawberry, sugar, and street food that did not belong on high-roller linen.
Mika watched him, then the dango, then the boy who had called her irreplaceable and immediately started eating like that was the end of the philosophy.
“…Give me one if you got extra,” she said. “If I’m a person, I get dango too.”
Curtis held the bag out without sitting up.
On the fifth floor, a reconstructed girl and a foreign student split sweets on separate beds —
and for a few minutes, Ikebukuro’s hunt stayed on the other side of a chained door.
Curtis chuckled and grabbed another stick.
Mika came over.
Not a run. Not a test. Just the quiet decision to leave the far bed and sit on the edge of his, white cap still on, cake abandoned for the moment. The suite’s careful geography — two beds, two sides, a locked door — bent without breaking.
He handed her the dango.
“Here.”
Their fingers did not make a ceremony of it. She took the stick, sat close enough that the mattress dipped, and bit into the first dumpling like she had not been chased by a Black Rider a couple of hours ago.
“…This is better than the cake,” she said around it, then glanced at him. “Don’t tell the chef.”
Curtis stayed on his back, long black braids on the pillow, mismatched eyes on the ceiling so she would not have to be stared at for sitting down.
“Secret’s safe.”
They ate like that for a stretch of hotel quiet. Two people on one bed, not touching more than the share required, the hunt paused at the chain on the door.
Mika looked at the dango, then at the boy who had called her irreplaceable and then simply handed her food.
“…Thanks,” she said again, smaller. “For not making me stay on the other side just because I came closer.”
Curtis tipped the bag toward her once more.
“You can sit. I’m not going anywhere.”
On the fifth floor, Mika Harima sat beside Curtis Nightstar with sugar on her fingers —
and the second bed stayed empty on purpose, not as a wall, just as a place she could return to if the kindness got too loud.
Curtis kept his eyes on the ceiling, easy.
“Whatever makes you feel comfortable. And it’s nice to see you ease up a bit.”
Mika glanced around the suite — two beds, city glass, leftover strawberry cake, a foreign student in a school uniform eating dango like he belonged here.
“…Are you rich or something?”
Curtis huffed a quiet sound.
“Not exactly. I just got a lot of money.” Long black braids shifted against the pillow. “The way it was obtained is not a savory story that is to be discussed while eating. However… maybe next time, when the storm settles, we can hang out and I’ll tell you.”
Mika looked at him over the dango stick.
“That’s a polite way of saying it’s bad.” Not pushing. Just clocking it. “You hide a girl, book a high-roller room, buy cake and dango, and then tell me the money’s a storm story for later. You’re weird.”
A short beat.
“Fine. Next time. If there is a next time that isn’t another alley.” She took another bite, then added, quieter, “I don’t need the savory part tonight. I just needed to know you’re not Yagiri money. This doesn’t feel like Yagiri money.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged.
“It isn’t.”
That was as much of the duffle bags and Justice and the list as a plate of sweets was going to get.
Mika nodded once, accepting the rain check the same way she had accepted Mika in this room.
On the fifth floor, the storm stayed outside.
The money stayed unexplained.
And for now, that was part of what made her feel comfortable enough to keep sitting on his bed instead of the far one.
Curtis leaned back into the pillows, dango stick idle between his fingers, and let the suite’s quiet put a different thought in his head.
Buying that car doesn’t seem so bad now.
Workshops. Hotels. Cake. A girl who needed off the street before the rider found her. Walking everywhere while half of Ikebukuro hunted a scar was starting to look like a luxury he did not have.
Elsewhere, Justice lost his mind on schedule.
Then use my money, dumbass!
A beat. The dead boy remembered the rules and groaned.
Aw, what am I even saying. You can’t hear me. I swear though — when I figure it out, I’m going to choke you for being a dumbass.
Curtis, who could not hear him, only stared at the ceiling and did the same math he always did: his bag was for living. Justice’s bag stayed shut. A memorial Gameboy was one thing. Spending a dead brother’s cash on a car because the city had gotten too fast felt like opening a grave to pay a cab fare.
Mika glanced at his face.
“…You went somewhere,” she said. Not accusing. Just noticing. “You do that. Eyes stay here. The rest of you leaves.”
Curtis blinked back into the room.
“Yeah. Just thinking about transportation. Nothing you gotta carry.”
Justice, unheard, pointed at the principle and the stubbornness and the second duffle bag like they were all the same crime.
On the fifth floor, Curtis Nightstar kept his brother’s money untouched —
and the city outside kept being a place that would have been easier with four wheels and a little less pride.
Curtis sat up and walked over to the bar.
The suite’s wet corner was all glass and dark wood and bottles lined up like they had never been needed for a chase. Long black braids shifted as he ran a look along the shelf, not pouring, just filing.
“Impressive. I’m going to need to remember this for myself next time.” A quiet huff. “This looks like a good getaway when I don’t want to be at home alone. Or I could build one. Hmmm.”
Mika watched him from the edge of his bed, dango still in her hand.
“…You hide a girl in a high-roller suite and your first renovation idea is a bar.” Not quite teasing. Close. “You’re planning a whole second house in your head while the city is still looking for me.”
Curtis tipped a bottle just enough to read the label, then set it back.
“Home gets loud. Sometimes you need a room that isn’t yours so your thoughts don’t think they own the lease.” He glanced back at her. “Not drinking. Just looking. You want water, it’s here too. Fancy ice and everything.”
He left the alcohol where it was and pulled two glasses anyway, filling them from the carafe like the bar had a civilian setting.
Mika accepted the water when he came back.
“If you build one,” she said, quieter, “don’t make it a hiding place that only works for you. That’s how people disappear and call it a getaway.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged.
“Noted.”
He sat again, workshop flyer still in his pocket, memorial box still waiting, a possible future bar sitting in his head beside a car he would not buy with Justice’s money —
and Mika Harima drank hotel water in a room that was already both a rescue and a preview of how Curtis Nightstar survived being alone.
Curtis turned the water glass in his hands.
“I’m already a missing-poster kid.” The words came out even, like he was reading a file that had his face on it. Long black braids shifted against the blue-shadowed hotel light. “And I disappeared from L.A. My friends don’t even know if I’m alive or where I went. So disappearing has been my specialty.”
Mika went still on the edge of the bed.
“…So you’re going to disappear tonight?”
The question had a chase in it. Doors. Riders. Boys who stayed until the useful part was over.
Curtis shook his head once.
“No.” He looked at her when he said it. “I was abandoned when I was five. Something happened a month back, and I just left the USA without telling anyone.”
He did not name the something. The month was enough. The memorial flyer in his pocket was enough.
Mika studied his face like she was checking whether no was another reconstruction.
“…You ran after someone left you,” she said quietly. “I ran so someone would keep me. That’s not the same disappearing. But it looks the same from the street.”
A short breath.
“If you do that thing you do — the thing where your eyes stay and the rest of you leaves — don’t do it out the door. I can handle a sad story. I can’t handle waking up to an empty suite and a keycard that doesn’t open anything anymore.”
Curtis tipped his chin toward his own bed.
“I’m here tonight. That’s not a rumor. That’s the arrangement.”
Mika nodded once, small.
On the fifth floor, two missing people sat with water and sweets —
one who had been erased from a forest at five and a country at nineteen,
one who had erased herself into a name that was not hers —
and neither of them, for this hour, was going to disappear.
Curtis’s gaze drifted back to the bar.
The bottles sat in a neat line, labels turned out like they were applying for a job. He whistled, low and appreciative, the same sound he had given Namie’s car before the gun came out.
“Some of these look good.” Long black braids shifted as he tipped his head, filing names he would not pour tonight. “Hmm. Some things to add to my shopping list. Heh.”
Mika followed his look, then snorted under her breath.
“You just told me you vanished from another country and now you’re window-shopping whiskey.” She pointed the empty dango stick at him. “Your moods have gears.”
Curtis shrugged, unbothered.
“Can’t drink the sad story. Can remember a bottle for later. Different shelves.” He left the row untouched. “Not opening any of it. Fancy room doesn’t mean I turn this into a bar fight with myself. Just… taking notes.”
Mika shook her head, almost fond despite herself.
“Add cake to that list too. The strawberry one. If you’re going to build a getaway, stock it like a person lives there, not like a ghost with expensive taste.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged.
“Cake. Dango. Two Gameboys. A bottle I won’t open tonight.” He glanced at her. “Shopping list’s getting longer.”
On the fifth floor, the hunt stayed outside.
Inside, Curtis Nightstar built a future out of labels and sweets —
and for a moment the missing-poster boy looked like someone who still planned on being around to buy the things he wrote down.
Curtis smiled, small and unforced.
“I’ll get more cake and dango if you plan to hang out with me.”
Mika stared at him like the offer had arrived in the wrong language.
Not a lab.
Not a condition on her face.
Not be Celty and he stays.
Cake. Dango. Hang out.
“…You’re bribing me with food,” she said, but there was no heat in it. The white cap brim dipped as she looked at the empty box, then at him. “That’s the least threatening deal I’ve been offered in months.”
A short breath.
“I can hang out. When the storm settles. When I’m not a girl a rider and a bartender are hunting. When I figure out how to be Mika in a room that isn’t locked.” She glanced at his mismatched eyes. “Don’t make it a promise I have to look pretty for. If I show up as myself and you still bring sweets, then it’s real.”
Curtis tipped his chin once.
“That’s the deal. You show up. I bring the food. No reconstruction required.”
Mika’s mouth tugged, almost a smile she did not trust yet.
“…Okay. Then I’ll hang out. Next time. After this suite stops feeling like the only safe place in Ikebukuro.”
On the fifth floor, a missing girl accepted a future made of strawberry cake and dango —
and Curtis Nightstar put it on the list beside Gameboys, a bar he might build, and a car he still would not buy with his brother’s money.
Curtis let the smile stay on his face for Mika and ran a different conversation behind it.
I’m going to need help on this.
The suite could hold one night. It could not hold a reconstructed girl, a Black Rider, Shizuo, Namie Yagiri, and a missing-person thread to Anri all by itself.
I can’t exactly ask Anri.
She was still looking. She deserved to know her friend was alive. She was also the last person he should drop a fifth-floor reunion on while Mika was still shaking over her own name.
Celty is off the table.
She had texted him to hunt this exact scar. Bringing her in now was handing Mika to the chase.
Namie is a definite no-go.
He had promised to keep an eye out for Seiji, not deliver the girl Seiji had run with into a Yagiri car.
…Fuck sakes.
He stared at the ceiling a second longer.
Wait.
Mikado.
Small-town kid. Same class. Knew Anri. Knew Seiji had walked out on day one. Not a hunter. Not a lab. Not a dullahan. Not a sister with an open wound where a brother should be. A boy who wanted to be in the city’s current without fully understanding how sharp it was — which made him dangerous in one way and usable in another.
Curtis did not look at Mika when the name landed.
Mikado Ryuugamine was not a plan yet.
He was the first name that was not automatically a disaster.
On the fifth floor, strawberry cake and dango sat between two missing people.
In Curtis Nightstar’s head, the list of people he could not call kept growing —
and the one name left on it was a classmate who still thought Ikebukuro was something you could walk into and survive by being kind.
Mika’s voice came smaller than the suite.
“Curtis? Can you say my name again?”
He tilted his head, long black braids shifting against the pillow, mismatched eyes finding her without making it a test.
“Sure, Mika. Everything okay?”
Her heart skipped.
Not because the name was new. Because he said it like it belonged in the room — no lab, no rider, no Seiji standing behind it waiting to correct her back into Celty.
She looked at her hands, then at him.
“…Yeah. I just needed to hear it twice in a row from somebody who isn’t trying to take it off me.” A short, embarrassed breath. “It sounds different when you say it. Like I’m not in trouble for having it.”
A thinner beat.
“You can go back to your shopping list and your bottles. I just… don’t let the next person who talks to me be the first one to say it after you. I want this version to last a little longer.”
Curtis nodded once.
“Mika.” Soft. Deliberate. A third time, because she had asked with her whole chest and not quite known she was asking. “I’m here. Name’s yours.”
She sat with that, heart still off-beat, strawberry sugar on her fingers, and for a moment the reconstruction was only a face —
not a replacement,
not a hunt,
just the girl he had called by her name and asked if she was okay.
Curtis turned back toward the bar.
The bottles stayed neatly aligned. His face stayed easy. Behind it, the plan kept chewing.
Mikado could be a key player to help.
Same class. Knew Anri. Knew Seiji had walked. Not a hunter.
But he’s also a liability.
Small-town kid. Jealous. Curious in the way that got people standing in the wrong street at the wrong hour. One conversation could become a Dollars rumor before breakfast.
I might just have to take my chances.
He has Masaomi, who could be a big help.
Masaomi knew the city’s teeth. Masaomi also knew how to keep a friend from walking into them — when he wanted to.
How do I get ahold of Mikado and him.
Maybe Anri-chan can help.
Not with the suite. Not with the face. Just a bridge. A message. A meeting that did not start with a Black Rider on the elevator.
He ran a finger along the edge of the bar and did not pour.
Behind him, Mika’s heart was in knots.
She watched the line of his shoulders, the braids, the way he had said her name like it was allowed, the way he had brought cake and left her a key and promised he would not disappear tonight. The way he asked if she was okay and waited for the real answer.
That.
That was the thing she had wanted Seiji to be.
Not a spotlight on a neck.
Not a love that required surgery to keep.
A person who stayed in the room and used her name and bought strawberry cake because she had asked for something sweet.
The thought scared her more than the rider.
Wanting Curtis to be what Seiji was not felt like a betrayal and a rescue at the same time. She looked down at the keycard in her lap so he would not see it on her face.
Curtis kept his eyes on the bottles.
Mika kept her new, dangerous comparison to herself.
On the fifth floor, two different silences occupied the same suite —
one making a list of classmates who might help keep a missing girl alive,
one realizing the boy at the bar had already become the standard a Yagiri brother was going to be measured against.
